    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive outlook, anchored by three standout plays. **Yordan Alvarez** (80.32) remains our premier start, benefiting from a high-upside matchup against Rea and a robust 0.419 xwOBA that cements his role as our primary run producer. While **Alvarez** brings the power, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (78.10) is our most critical asset for category scarcity today; facing Teng, **Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 14% platoon edge and the high-leverage leadoff spot, making him our highest-projected source of stolen bases. **Christian Yelich** (74.33) also earns an elite designation, as the combination of Sasaki\u2019s 5.11 xERA and a significant platoon advantage positions him perfectly to capitalize in the cleanup spot.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Caleb Durbin**, whose profile was strong enough to challenge the utility slot; however, his absence from the official starting lineup rendered his score null, forcing us to prioritize the locked-in production of **Pete Crow-Armstrong**. We are also closely monitoring **Trea Turner** (68.35). Despite a 5% wind-in penalty at his park, **Turner** remains a locked-in start against Cecconi. His score is bolstered by a 4% efficiency boost stemming from the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a unique stolen base opportunity that offsets the unfavorable weather conditions. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, particularly regarding our depth. We have effectively mitigated exposure to elite arms by keeping **Kazuma Okamoto** benched against the dominant Skenes, protecting our efficiency caps. Meanwhile, the rainout of **Junior Caminero** is a notable blow to our middle-infield ceiling, but the depth provided by **Chase Meidroth** (56.97) offers a stable floor. With **Gary Sanchez** (56.63) and **Luke Raley** (54.13) both holding steady in favorable matchups, we are well-positioned to maintain our category leads provided the wind stays within projected parameters for **Kyle Schwarber** and **Trea Turner**.",
